# RetrieveNotificationsList against this project's own test eUICC,
# captured with rsp_record_open.
#
# The five signed counterparts of omnikey-notifications.log's five
# metadata entries. All of them are profileInstallationResult (tag BF37);
# otherSignedNotification only appears for enable, disable and delete,
# which this card has never been asked to report.
#
# Their sizes are a cross-check worth keeping: the last two are 163
# bytes, which is exactly what `euicc card install --server` printed as
# the length of the ProfileInstallationResult the card handed back at
# install time. The queue holds the same bytes the install returned
# inline -- which is what makes slicing them out of the response, rather
# than re-encoding a decode, the only correct way to carry them.
#
# The first three are 186 bytes: installs from before euicc-rsp took a
# real SM-DP+ address, so their notificationAddress is
# smdp-address-placeholder.invalid and they can never be delivered.
# A euicc-lpa session, recorded by rsp_record_open: one line per
# direction, hex APDUs (see this project's src/rsp_transport.c for the
# exact format). This is a log, not something to run.
#
# Whether this file is safe to share depends on what was asked of the
# card during the session it captures, and this header cannot know
# that in general -- a read-only query (EID, versions, key
# identifiers) is public; a write session (installing, enabling,
# deleting a profile) can carry protected material. Check what is
# actually below before pasting this into an issue.
